# =============================================================================
# functio — An unhandled failure directly in main panics
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `main` is the top frame: a failable call there (`→ T ⇥ E`) needs no
#     handler. If it fails, the program panics — exit code 1, the error value
#     reported as the message — instead of being a compile error.
#   • Output before the failing call is kept: `3` below prints, `99` never does.
#   • Only `main` is exempt. A helper function calling a failable function still
#     needs `fac { } cape err { }` or its own `⇥`: errors do not unwind through
#     a frame that does not declare them.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • Expecting the panic to be recoverable with `fac … cape` around `main`.
#   • Relying on this in a helper — the same call there is
#     `failable_call_requires_handler`.
#
# See also: functio, ⇥, fac, cape, iace
# =============================================================================

# functio — unhandled failable call in main
#
# POLICY:
#   The call below runs on the happy path first (`risky(1)` is 2, so the sum
#   is 3), then `risky(-1)` throws "negative input" with no handler in sight:
#   the program aborts with that message.

fn risky(int n) → int ⇥ string {
    if n ≺ 0 {
        throw "negative input"
    }
    return n + 1
}

main {
    print risky(1) + 1
    let int x ← risky(-1)
    print x
    print 99
}
